Every storage platform has a query it dreads. Ours was one of the most
innocent-looking calls in the API: list my buckets, with sizes.

For most of Tigris's life, answering it was the single largest source of
expensive list traffic in the system. Not object reads. Not multipart uploads. A
dashboard page. Here's why that query cost so much, why the obvious fixes were
wrong, and how we made it cheap with a cache whose interesting engineering isn't
the cache at all. It's proving exactly how stale the answer can ever be.

## Why a bucket size costs 1,280 reads

Tigris keeps all of its metadata — tenants, buckets, IAM, billing, object rows —
in FoundationDB, so every metadata operation is an ordinary ACID transaction.
That includes usage accounting. Every PUT, DELETE, and multipart completion
adjusts a set of counters for the bucket it touches: row counts, plus
per-storage-tier byte sizes for both committed and staged data.

If those counters were single keys, every write to a hot bucket would contend on
the same handful of FoundationDB keys, and a busy bucket would serialize on its
own bookkeeping. So they aren't single keys. Each logical counter is sharded
across 128 keys, and a write bumps one shard atomically. Writes scale with no
hotspots and no contention.

The bill arrives on the read side. Reconstructing one bucket's size means
reading every shard of every counter: roughly 10 counters × 128 shards ≈ **1,280
reads per bucket**, inside a transaction. The metadata for a whole page of
buckets, by comparison, is a single range read. The sizes dwarf everything else
in the response.

Now put that behind a dashboard. Fly.io is our largest platform partner, and
their extension's `ListBuckets` handler calls our backend with `with_size=true`.
A tenant with ~500 buckets triggers 500 × 1,280 ≈ **640,000 FoundationDB reads
per page load**. At Fly's call volume, this one path dominated list-buckets
latency platform-wide.

