The galling part: almost none of it was necessary. Most buckets on any given
dashboard load received zero writes since the last load. Their sizes hadn't
changed. We were recomputing identical numbers, at five-figure read cost per
page, over and over.

## The insight that licensed the fix

The obvious responses are "make the computation faster" or "precompute sizes
into a single key." Both attack the compute path, and the compute path isn't the
problem. The problem is recomputing values that haven't changed.

What licensed a cache was a property the metric already had: **a bucket size is
an approximation the moment it's computed.** Writes race the counter scan;
replication lags; staged bytes come and go. Callers of this path already receive
eventually-consistent numbers. Serving "approximately right, as of ten minutes
ago" instead of "approximately right, as of now" changes nothing a caller could
correctly rely on.

That observation did real design work. An early draft added a `with_cached_size`
opt-in flag to the request proto, plus a "force fresh" escape hatch. Review
killed both: if the number is approximate either way, a knob choosing which
approximation is API surface without a purpose. The cache became an invisible
gateway-side intercept. Callers change nothing, and enablement is a
per-namespace feature flag.

## The design: cache at the edge, miss-fill in bulk

The mechanism lives entirely in the gateway, in front of the backend that owns
the counters. We already run a regional edge cache — KVRocks, a RocksDB-backed
store speaking the Redis protocol, shared by all gateway pods in a region and
persistent across deploys — so the bucket-size cache is a new table in an
existing store, not new infrastructure.

The read path, when a size-augmented list arrives for an enabled namespace:

1. The gateway rewrites the backend call to `with_size=false` — a cheap,
   metadata-only range scan.
2. It looks up each returned bucket in the edge cache. Hits merge into the
   response directly.
3. For the misses on the page, it issues **one** follow-up RPC:
   `GetTenantBuckets(names=[the missed ones], with_size=true)`. The backend
   point-reads exactly those buckets, runs the counter scan for each, and
   returns them. The gateway caches each result with a jittered multi-day TTL
   and completes the page.

The `names` filter on that request is the only proto change in the entire
project, and it's independently useful for any point-lookup tooling.

