The bulk miss-fill matters more than it looks. A naive cache does one backend
call per missed bucket; a 500-bucket page at 90% hit rate would fire 50 RPCs.
Ours fires one RPC per page, sizing ~50 buckets instead of 500. And because the
cached value is the full `BucketMetadata` message rather than a hand-projected
size struct, there's no second schema to keep in sync. On a hit, a helper grafts
the cached size fields onto the fresh listing, and recreate detection (more on
that below) compares `created_at` timestamps that were already there.

## Invalidation without inventing state

Caching is the easy 20%. The defensible engineering is in the other 80%: how do
you serve a value with a four-day TTL and still promise it's never meaningfully
wrong?

The answer has two layers, and the elegant part is what we didn't build. There
is no invalidation queue, no tombstone table, no per-bucket cooldown record.
There is no new state primitive anywhere.

**Layer one: invalidation is a TTL-shorten, not a delete.** When a size-changing
write lands — PUT, DeleteObjects, CopyObject, rename, multipart completion — the
gateway shortens the cached entry's remaining TTL to a cooldown window (default
15 minutes), and only if the remaining TTL is currently longer. The trick is
that the entry's remaining TTL is itself the cooldown clock. A long TTL means
"not invalidated recently — shorten it." A short one means "already invalidated
within the window — do nothing." One conditional expire encodes the entire
rate-limiting state machine, idempotently, across every gateway pod. (One
operational wrinkle: the KVRocks fork we deploy doesn't support Redis's
`EXPIRE ... LT`, so the shorten is a TTL read plus conditional `Expire`. The
property survives; the one-liner didn't.)

**Layer two: a per-pod debounce guards the hot path.** A bucket taking 100
writes per second would otherwise issue 100 cache operations per second,
dwarfing the recomputation the cache exists to elide. An in-memory LRU keyed by
bucket collapses a write storm to roughly one cache operation per cooldown
window per pod, with no network round-trip on the suppressed writes.
Cross-region writes ride an event that already existed for object-cache
invalidation — the debounce is checked before the namespace resolution, so a
remote write storm collapses in memory too. No new event type, no producer
change.

The result is a system whose staleness is bounded twice over, and the bounds are
the point:

- **Busy bucket:** at most ~one cooldown window (15 minutes) stale, sustained.
  Each cooldown expiry lets the next write shorten again, so a
  continuously-written bucket re-converges on schedule, forever.
- **Missed invalidation entirely** — a dropped event, a bug, a network
  partition: bounded by the jittered multi-day TTL. The TTL is not the
  mechanism; it's the insurance against the mechanism failing.

