## Fifteen ways to be wrong, and one race we kept

A multi-day TTL is only safe if you've enumerated how it can lie to you. The
design doc works through fifteen corner cases; three earn their place in any
honest writeup.

**Delete and recreate.** Delete bucket `photos`, create a new `photos`. The
hard-delete path emits no cache event — we verified this in the code rather than
assuming it — so a stale size could surface for the newborn bucket for days. The
defense costs nothing: the cached value already carries the bucket's
`created_at`, the fresh listing carries the new one, and a mismatch is treated
as a miss. Same-name recreate gets a genuine recompute automatically.

**Burst, then silence.** A write burst shortens the TTL once; further writes in
the window are suppressed. If writes then stop entirely, no future event will
ever fire — and without care, the entry would serve its pre-burst size until the
full TTL expired, days later. The shorten-not-delete design absorbs this: the
entry now expires within minutes of the burst regardless, and the next read
refills fresh. Worst-case silent staleness fell from days to roughly two
cooldown windows.

**The fill/invalidate race — which we chose not to fix.** A miss-fill is
dispatched at T0; a write lands at T1 while the fill is in flight; the fill
returns at T2 and caches a value computed before the write, under a fresh TTL.
We considered generation counters, tombstones, and CAS, and rejected them all.
The race window is the tens of milliseconds between dispatch and return; the
staleness ceiling it produces is the same TTL ceiling we already accept for a
missed event; and the metric is approximate by contract. Adding coordination
machinery to an eventually-consistent number is complexity spent where
correctness doesn't live. We documented the race and kept it.

## What the spec got wrong

The design doc in our repo carries an unusual section: as-built annotations
recording where the implementation diverged from the design, and why. The diffs
are the most instructive part.

The original invalidation design stored a `last_modified` timestamp inside the
cached value as the cooldown anchor, with delete-plus-refill semantics. Review
and implementation replaced it with the TTL-shorten plus debounce described
above — simpler, one less field, and the burst-then-silence case falls out for
free instead of needing a special clamp. The spec's design remains in the
document as a record of the road not taken.

We also added a tenant-ID field to the cross-region cache event, then reverted
it one PR later. The event historically carried only the bucket name, and the
cache key needs a namespace — but review pushed back: the namespace is
resolvable from the bucket at handle time, so it doesn't need to ride every
write event in the system. Growing a hot-path event payload to save one cached
lookup in a cold handler is the wrong trade, and it took a reviewer to say so.

Specs are hypotheses. The honest changelog is worth more than the spec.

## The arithmetic, after

For a typical 500-bucket page at a steady-state hit rate above 90%: one cheap
metadata range read, one batched cache lookup, and one bulk miss-fill sizing ~50
buckets instead of 500 — with each of those 50 amortized over days of subsequent
hits. The design target from the team discussion was blunt: take roughly a
million expensive size queries down to about a hundred, four orders of
magnitude, while keeping staleness provably bounded at fifteen minutes for busy
buckets and the TTL for pathological failures.

