The cache outlives its own backend, too. The sharded-counter scan behind the
miss-fill is slated for replacement by an OLAP-backed size path — and when that
lands, only the miss-fill's implementation swaps. The cache table, the
TTL-shorten invalidation, the debounce, the staleness bounds: all of it carries
over, because none of it ever depended on how the number was computed. Even with
a fast analytical backend, there's no reason to ask it about buckets that
haven't changed.

## Takeaways

Three things we'd tell anyone building on sharded counters, in FoundationDB or
anywhere else.

**The design that makes writes scale is the design that makes reads expensive.**
Sharding a counter doesn't eliminate the cost of aggregation; it defers it to
whoever reads. Know which side of that trade your traffic lives on before your
dashboard becomes your most expensive endpoint.

**An approximate metric is a caching license — take it.** If callers can't
distinguish "as of now" from "as of the cooldown window," refusing to cache is
pure waste. And resist adding a force-fresh knob for the caller who
theoretically wants one; they don't, and the API surface is forever.

**The cache is not the engineering. The staleness bounds are.** Anyone can put
Redis in front of a slow query. The work that makes it safe is the enumeration:
fifteen corner cases, two provable bounds, and one race consciously accepted,
written down where the next engineer can check it.

